@poteto's pstack is built for cursor cloud agents, but you can use your codex and claude subscriptions too! i made onigiri-mode (using automate-me) to use opus 5.5 and sol 6.1 as competing models, with claude code and pi (with codex sub) as the tools of choice

compared to naively asking grokbot to "just use pstack with claude code and pi", i clanked around a little and saw some serious efficiency improvements (note: tiny sample size, so take it with a grain of salt):

- cut claude code token usage by 54% (191k to 87.5k)
- wakes the orchestrator about 94% less (83 to 5)
- reduces cron polling per PR by 98% (55 to 1)

@bot seems to have a tendency to start polling for just about anything, which can be resolved by forcing it to use hooks and a more event-driven structure.

Claude Code tip, and it's f*cking easy to miss: Opus 5.5 ignores a top-level effortLevel in your user settings

Opus 5.5 starts at medium, and an effortLevel at the top level of ~/.claude/settings.json doesn't count for it. that older key still applies to models like Opus 5 and Fable 5.1

the easy fix: while Opus 5.5 is your active model, run /effort, pick a level and confirm with Enter, not s. Claude Code saves it for that model under modelSettings. s applies it to this session only

or write it yourself in ~/.claude/settings.json. if you already have a modelSettings object, add only the claude-opus-5-5 entry to it. if not, create it (and delete the old top-level line if Opus 5.5 is the only model you use):

"modelSettings": {

"claude-opus-5-5": {

"effortLevel": "high"

}

}

then run /effort status, or read the session header, which shows the effort next to the model name

if it still isn't what you set, two things can sit above it. CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL outranks your saved settings and a subagent's own effort: run echo $CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL, and look for the same name in the env block of settings.json. and an admin can cap the level with the managed maxEffortLevel setting

Sonnet 5.5 and Haiku 5.5 start at medium too. switch to each and run /effort status

